    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Preliminary mapping of satellite images of Lavinia Planitia, Venus, shows regions where extensional fractures are concentrated along portions of ridge belts, displaying geometries similar to secondary structures such as tail cracks formed during strike-slip faulting. This research investigates the hypothesis that the extensional features are formed as the result of strike-slip faulting localized along pre-existing ridge belts. This problem has been idealized in terms of the Eshelby model for analyzing elastic inhomogeneities, which allows for the computation of deformation fields around an inhomogeneity under various remote loading conditions. This poster summarizes initial findings from mapping and outlines the Eshelby approach to the problem.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
